---
/**
 * Where a page's first screen ends: the first paint waits until the page has arrived this far.
 *
 * A slow connection hands a page over in pieces, and the browser paints what it has: half a hero. Whatever is
 * centred (words beside a photo, a row of logos, two founders side by side) then moved when the rest arrived,
 * by up to 110px. With firstScreenEnd on the layout (BaseLayout, or PageLayout which passes it on), the page's
 * head says <link rel="expect" href="#first-screen-end" blocking="render">, and Chrome paints only once this marker
 * has arrived: the first screen is whole from the first paint. Put it, once and never inside a condition, right
 * after the last thing on a laptop's first screen. A layout told to wait for a marker the page doesn't have would
 * wait for the whole page (tests/first-screen.test.mjs checks the two go together).
 *
 * Checked in Chrome, Nov 11 2026: a slow phone paints about 40ms later, and nothing on the first screen moves.
 */
---

<span id="first-screen-end" hidden></span>
